             [--LL _p_r_e_f_i_x/_p_r_e_f_l_e_n,_i_f_1[,_i_f_2,_._._.]] [--NN _i_f_1[,_i_f_2,_._._.]]
             [--OO _p_r_e_f_i_x/_p_r_e_f_l_e_n,_i_f_1[,_i_f_2,_._._.]] [--TT _i_f_1[,_i_f_2,_._._.]] [--tt _t_a_g]

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The rroouuttee66dd utility is a routing daemon which supports RIP over IPv6.

     The options are as follows:

     --AA _p_r_e_f_i_x/_p_r_e_f_l_e_n,_i_f_1[,_i_f_2,_._._.]
             This option is used for aggregating routes.  _p_r_e_f_i_x/_p_r_e_f_l_e_n spec‐
             ifies the prefix and the prefix length of the aggregated route.
             When advertising routes, rroouuttee66dd filters specific routes covered
             by the aggregate and advertises the aggregated route
             _p_r_e_f_i_x/_p_r_e_f_l_e_n to the interfaces specified in the comma-separated
             interface list _i_f_1[,_i_f_2,_._._.].  rroouuttee66dd creates a static route to
             _p_r_e_f_i_x/_p_r_e_f_l_e_n, with the RTF_REJECT flag set, into the kernel
             routing table.

     --aa      Enables aging of the statically defined routes.  With this op‐
             tion, any statically defined routes will be removed unless corre‐
             sponding updates arrive as if the routes are received at the
             startup of rroouuttee66dd.

     --DD      Enables extensive output of debugging messages.  This option also
             instructs rroouuttee66dd to run in foreground mode (i.e. it does not
             become a daemon process).

     --dd      Enables output of debugging messages.  This option also instructs
             rroouuttee66dd to run in foreground mode (i.e. it does not become a
             daemon process).

     --hh      Disables split horizon processing.

     --LL _p_r_e_f_i_x/_p_r_e_f_l_e_n,_i_f_1[,_i_f_2,_._._.]
             Filter incoming routes from interfaces _i_f_1[,_i_f_2,_._._.].  rroouuttee66dd
             will accept incoming routes that are in _p_r_e_f_i_x/_p_r_e_f_l_e_n.  If mul‐
             tiple --LL options are specified, all routes that match any of the
             options are accepted.  ::/0 is treated specially as the default
             route, not “any route that has longer prefix length than, or
             equal to, 0”.  For example, with “-L 2001:db8::/32,if1 -L
             ::/0,if1”, rroouuttee66dd will accept the default route and routes in
             the 2001:db8::/32 address range, but no others.  To accept any
             route, simply do not specify the --LL option.

     --ll      By default, rroouuttee66dd will not exchange site local routes for
             safety reasons.  This is because the semantics of site local ad‐
             dress space are rather vague, as the specification is still being
             worked on, and there is no good way to define the site local
             boundary.  With --ll, rroouuttee66dd will exchange site local routes as
             well.  It must not be used on site boundary routers, since --ll as‐
             sumes that all interfaces are in the same site.

     --NN _i_f_1[,_i_f_2,_._._.]
             Do not listen to, or advertise, route from/to interfaces speci‐
             fied by _i_f_1[,_i_f_2,_._._.].

     --nn      Do not update the kernel routing table.

     --OO _p_r_e_f_i_x/_p_r_e_f_l_e_n,_i_f_1[,_i_f_2,_._._.]
             Restrict route advertisement toward interfaces specified by
             _i_f_1[,_i_f_2,_._._.].  With this option rroouuttee66dd will only advertise
             routes that match _p_r_e_f_i_x/_p_r_e_f_l_e_n.

     --qq      Makes rroouuttee66dd use listen-only mode.  No advertisement is sent.

     --SS      This option is the same as --ss, except that the split horizon rule
             does apply.

     --ss      Makes rroouuttee66dd advertise the statically defined routes which exist
             in the kernel routing table when rroouuttee66dd is invoked.  Announce‐
             ments obey the regular split horizon rule.

     --TT _i_f_1[,_i_f_2,_._._.]
             Advertise only the default route toward _i_f_1[,_i_f_2,_._._.].

     --tt _t_a_g  Attach the route tag _t_a_g to originated route entries.  _t_a_g can be
             decimal, octal prefixed by 0, or hexadecimal prefixed by 0x.

     --uu      Always log route updates (insertions and deletions).  Route up‐
             dates are always prefixed with “RTADD” or “RTDEL”.

     Upon receipt of signal SIGINT or SIGUSR1, rroouuttee66dd will log a dump of the
     current internal state.

